@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        Let the man who seeks to be justified through Christ, by God's unmerited goodness, 
        consider that this cannot be attained without him taking at the same time 
        sanctification, or, in other words, being renewed to innocence and purity of life.
    </span>
    <span>John Calvin, Commentary on 1 Corinthians</span>
</div>

<ol>
    <li>
        Sanctification is an evangelical grace wherein God infuses a person with the righteousness of Christ,
        so that their life and habits are transformed, purified, and cleansed of sin.
    </li>
    <li>
        Sanctification is possible only through Christ, and by means of a faith that joyfully receives, responds to,
        and cooperates with the transformative work of the Holy Spirit.
    </li>
    <li>
        Though sanctification renews a person's mind, body, habits, and actions, it is not a result of human effort.
        Man's inner transformation is given as a gracious gift from the Father, worked out by the Holy Spirit, and in
        pursuit of Christ.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />